---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { xai, supermajority2025 } from "../data/history";

const tesla10q = "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000162828026066775/tsla-20260930.htm";
const spacex10q = "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1181412/000162828026066880/spcx-20260930.htm";
const charter = "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000162828024032662/tsla-2024x06x30xex31.htm";
const tboc457 = "https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/BO/htm/BO.21.htm#21.457";
const tboc365 = "https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/BO/htm/BO.21.htm#21.365";
---

<Base
  title="The vote"
  description="What the October 22, 2026 filings say about a possible combination, and why the voting threshold is unconfirmed until a proxy is filed."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Unconfirmed until a proxy is filed</p>
    <h1>What is known, and what would have to be voted.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two special committees are looking. Nobody has published terms. This page stays inside the
      filings, the Texas statute, and the certificate of formation already on EDGAR.
    </p>

    <h2>What the filings say</h2>
    <p>
      The TSLA issuer’s Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026, filed October 22, 2026,
      says:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The Tesla board of directors has formed a special committee (the “Special Committee”)
        comprising disinterested directors to explore deepening Tesla’s relationship with SpaceX,
        which could be through certain arrangements including without limitation a business
        combination (a “Transaction”). While there could be merits to a Transaction, there is no
        assurance that any such Transaction will be consummated, as a Transaction would be subject
        to, among other things, the affirmative recommendation of the Special Committee and, if
        agreed upon, any necessary shareholder approval.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={tesla10q}>Tesla Form 10-Q, Item 5</a> · filed October 22, 2026
    </p>
    <p>SpaceX’s Form 10-Q, also filed October 22, 2026, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        As announced on our earnings call on October 20, 2026, our board of directors has formed a
        special committee to evaluate a potential combination between SpaceX and Tesla, Inc.
        ("Tesla"). The board of directors of Tesla has also formed a special committee. No
        determination by our special committee has been made regarding any potential combination,
        and any potential combination would be subject to further approval by our special committee,
        our board of directors and our controlling shareholder.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={spacex10q}>SpaceX Form 10-Q</a> · filed October 22, 2026 · quarter ended September 30, 2026
    </p>

    <h2>What is not in those filings</h2>
    <ul class="clean">
      <li>No price, exchange ratio, or structure.</li>
      <li>No names of the committee members, in the paragraphs quoted above.</li>
      <li>No proxy, no record date, and no meeting date for a combination.</li>
      <li>No statement of which vote — Texas default, charter, exchange rule, or something else — would apply.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The likely threshold</h2>
    <p><span class="badge">Unconfirmed for this transaction</span></p>
    <p>
      Texas Business Organizations Code section 21.457 says that, except as the code or the
      certificate of formation provides, approval of a fundamental business transaction takes the
      affirmative vote of at least <strong>two-thirds of the outstanding shares</strong> entitled to
      vote on it. Section 21.365 lets a certificate set a different portion, but not below a majority.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={tboc457}>TBOC § 21.457</a> ·
      <a href={tboc365}>TBOC § 21.365</a>
      · statute text as published by the Texas Legislature
    </p>
    <p>
      The certificate of formation signed June 13, 2024, the day of the conversion to Texas, says
      in section 4.3(a), subject to preferred-stock rights, the bylaws, and Article IX:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        the vote of shareholders holding a majority of the shares of stock entitled to vote on the
        matter then outstanding shall be sufficient to approve, authorize, adopt, or to otherwise
        cause the Corporation to take, or affirm the Corporation’s taking of, any action, including
        any “fundamental business transaction” as defined in the TBOC.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={charter}>Certificate of formation, Exhibit 3.1</a> · signed June 13, 2024
    </p>
    <p>
      Article IX of that same certificate is narrower than a merger. It requires the affirmative
      vote of at least 66 2/3% of the voting power of shares entitled to vote generally in director
      elections in order to amend, or adopt something inconsistent with, Article V, Article VI,
      Article VII, or Article IX. Those articles cover the board, who may amend the bylaws, meeting
      mechanics, and the amendment rule itself. The November 6, 2025 proposal to eliminate
      applicable supermajority requirements was not approved
      ({supermajority2025.counts}).
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={supermajority2025.href}>{supermajority2025.source}</a>
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Do not treat the charter sentence as the rule for a deal that does not exist yet.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        The form of any transaction is unknown. Preferred stock, a class vote, an exchange rule, or
        a later charter amendment could change the math. The proxy for that meeting is the document
        that states the standard. Until it is filed, the majority-of-outstanding reading is a
        reading of the June 13, 2024 certificate, not a confirmed voting item.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Why abstentions and silence matter</h2>
    <p>
      On a “majority of the shares then outstanding” standard, the denominator is shares outstanding,
      not shares that showed up. A yes vote is what counts toward the numerator. An abstention, a
      broker non-vote, and a share that is never returned do not add a yes. For getting over that
      line, they work like a no. They are not rewritten as “against” on the inspector’s sheet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Broker non-votes are the column Tesla’s meeting 8-Ks use for shares a broker did not vote.
      They are not yes votes in those tables.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shares outstanding are a separate, dated figure. The September 30, 2026 balance sheet is
      presented in millions and reports common stock of 3,953 and 3,751 shares issued and outstanding
      as of September 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025. Par on that line is $4 million and $3 million.
      At the certificate’s $0.001 par value, 3,953 million shares produce about $4 million of par,
      which matches the line. This page reads the figure as <strong>3,953 million shares</strong>
      outstanding as of September 30, 2026. It is not a record date for any combination vote.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={tesla10q}>Form 10-Q balance sheet</a> · as of September 30, 2026 ·
      <a href={charter}>par value in the certificate</a>
    </p>

    <h2>The xAI lesson</h2>
    <p>{xai.prose}</p>
    <p>Certified counts: {xai.counts}</p>
    <p class="source">
      <a href={xai.href}>{xai.source}</a> · meeting {xai.meeting}
    </p>
    <p>
      That proposal was advisory, and its counting rule was the bylaw standard the 8-K describes.
      A combination, if one is proposed, may use a different standard. The useful point is narrower:
      more yes votes than no votes is not always enough when abstentions count against the proposal,
      or when the hurdle is a majority of every share outstanding.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="button" href="/how-to-vote/">How a ballot actually gets cast</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
